<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- FORK-LOCAL PATCH (adrienlacombe/buzz): every user-visible name here is
         BitcoinMarkets, matching productName in tauri.conf.json.

         tauri.conf.json's productName only names the .app directory, the DMG and
         the mounted volume. These keys are what macOS actually shows: Finder uses
         CFBundleDisplayName, the menu bar beside the Apple logo uses
         CFBundleName, and the three usage descriptions are quoted verbatim in the
         system permission prompts. Left unpatched, the bundle was
         BitcoinMarkets.app while the app called itself Buzz everywhere a user
         could see it, and asked for the microphone as "Buzz".

         CFBundleIdentifier is deliberately NOT renamed — see AGENTS.md. Nor is
         the executable name (buzz-desktop): it is the Cargo binary, not a
         user-visible string.

         This file is otherwise byte-identical to upstream, so a sync that adds a
         permission string will conflict here. Resolution: take upstream's new key
         and rewrite its Buzz to BitcoinMarkets. -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>BitcoinMarkets</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>BitcoinMarkets</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>BitcoinMarkets needs microphone access for voice huddles.</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>BitcoinMarkets needs camera access to record animated avatars.</string>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>BitcoinMarkets uses your local network for optional Share Compute and local relay connections. Remote messaging does not require this access.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
